  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1202 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n \n\nFrom Monday night through Tuesday morning, light northerly winds are \nforecast to be in place at the surface with elevated moisture \narriving from the 850 MB to 700 MB layer. Overnight lows range from \nthe mid and upper 30s across higher elevations to mid 40s across the \nCoastal Plains. Then, during the day on Tuesday, another cold front \nis forecast to push across South Central Texas and brings chances of \nrain of 20 to 50 percent. The GEFS and EPS ensemble guidance \nsuggests 10 to 40 percent chance of rain exceeding 0.1 inch of \nrainfall, which tells me that rainfall amounts likely limited to a \ntrace to a few hundredths. Tuesday's high temperatures in the low \n50s across the southern Edwards Plateau and Hill Country to mid and \nupper 50s for the I-35 corridor, Coastal Plains, and low 60s for the \nRio Grande. Behind this front, a cooler airmass comes down to our \nlocal area with lows in the low 30s over portions of the southern \nEdwards Plateau and Hill Country. This could be the first freeze for \nthis fall season across those locations. Under sunny skies, a cool \nWednesday awaits with highs in the 50s areawide. Then, the night \ntime comes with clear skies and light winds allowing temperatures to \ndrop again to the low 30s across the southern Edwards Plateau and \nHill Country and mid to upper 30s elsewhere. Southerly flow returns \nfor Thanksgiving day, but cool temperatures stay across South \nCentral Texas with highs in the 50s.\n\nRain chances may come back on Friday for the Coastal Plains but \nconfidence on this scenario still on the air and decided go with no \nPoPs for now still a clearer picture takes place.",
